Observations and Interpretations

This document accompanies the Chronology of Events. It is intended to distinguish my observations, concerns, and interpretations from the factual chronology. The chronology is the primary record of what occurred. This document explains whyLater in July 2026 — Conversation with Austin

I was talking with Austin at the lunch table.

I asked, “How long have you been here?”

He replied, “A couple of days.”

I asked, “How was the transition?”

He replied, “It was pretty good.”

I said, “I just came back from Sacramento. The transitions were pretty difficult.”

He replied, “I was staying in another house.”

I asked, “Oh, how long have you known Phil?”

He replied, “About four days.”

I said, “I don’t understand. If you were at another house, how come you only know Phil for four days?”

He replied, “Oh, I was at the House of Hope.”

At that point, Vince came over to the table. He stood with his fists clenched at his sides and said,

“Alex, never, ever, ever do that again. Do you understand me? We do not ask personal questions in this house. We do not ask where people are from. We do not ask anything personal. Do I make myself clear, Alex?”

He then asked,

“Does this shock you, Alex?”

Vince then referred to Austin and said,

“We are taking care of him, not you. We are handling this, not you.”

I was surprised by his response because I believed I had been engaged in an ordinary conversation with a new resident about his transition to the house. My questions followed naturally from what Austin had told me. I believe particular events are significant.

Repeated Pattern

As I reviewed the chronology, I observed what I believe is a recurring pattern of public correction, criticism, or ridicule during ordinary interactions. My interpretation is based on the cumulative sequence of events rather than on any single incident.

Conversation with Austin

During my conversation with Austin, Vince interrupted and stated:

"Alex, never, ever, ever do that again."

He later added:

"We are taking care of him, not you. We are handling this, not you."

My interpretation of these statements was that Vince was communicating that Austin required care that staff, rather than residents, should provide. Vince did not explicitly state that Austin had a mental illness or that he was providing professional mental health treatment. That conclusion is my interpretation, not a statement of fact.

Medication Disclosure

During the Adult Protective Services meeting, Vince stated:

"You always say, 'Oh, I need my medication. I need my medication.'"

I interpreted this as indicating that Vince knew I was taking psychiatric medication and chose to disclose that information during the meeting in front of Phil and the Adult Protective Services caseworker.

When I asked,

"Who told you I take medication? How do you know I take medication? It's none of your business."

I do not recall receiving an answer.

I found this upsetting because I consider my psychiatric treatment to be private medical information.

Different Standards

Several events caused me to question whether the same standards were being applied consistently to different residents. Examples include meal scheduling, the handling of water for coffee, and noise during meals. These observations arise from multiple incidents described in the chronology rather than any single event.

Effect on Me

I believe the cumulative effect of these interactions contributed to increased stress and reduced sleep. During this period, my sleep decreased substantially, and I experienced symptoms that I believe were consistent with hypomania. After my psychiatric medication was adjusted, my sleep improved to approximately seven to eight hours per night.

I cannot state that every event in the chronology directly caused these changes. Rather, my concern is that the cumulative stress described in the chronology contributed to my overall decline in well-being and judgment during that period.

